================================================================================
    SECTION 1: THE SWING TRADING FRAMEWORK
================================================================================

Swing trading captures intermediate price moves lasting 3-20 trading days. This
timeframe sits in the sweet spot between the noise of day trading and the
patience required for position trading. It allows working professionals to trade
actively without watching screens all day.

CORE PHILOSOPHY: Buy stocks that are pulling back within an established uptrend,
and sell stocks that are bouncing within an established downtrend. You are not
predicting reversals. You are joining momentum after a temporary pause.

TIMEFRAME HIERARCHY:
    Weekly chart: Determines the primary trend direction
    Daily chart: Identifies pullback zones and entry setups
    4-Hour chart: Fine-tunes entry timing (optional)

MARKET REGIME FILTER: Before taking any trade, assess the overall market
environment using the S&P 500 and Nasdaq Composite.

    BULLISH REGIME: SPY above its 50-day and 200-day moving averages with the
    50 above the 200. Take long setups aggressively. Avoid shorts.

    BEARISH REGIME: SPY below both moving averages with the 50 below the 200.
    Focus on short setups or stay in cash. Avoid longs.

    CHOPPY/NEUTRAL: SPY is range-bound between the moving averages. Reduce
    position sizes by 50% and only take the highest-conviction setups.

================================================================================
    SECTION 2: ENTRY RULES AND SETUP IDENTIFICATION
================================================================================

THE PULLBACK BUY SETUP (Long Trades):

All five conditions must be met before entering:

    1. TREND CONFIRMATION: Stock must be above its rising 20-day and 50-day
       exponential moving averages (EMA) on the daily chart.

    2. PULLBACK ZONE: Price has pulled back to the 10-day or 20-day EMA after
       a swing high. The pullback should retrace 30-60% of the prior swing.

    3. VOLUME CONTRACTION: During the pullback, volume should decrease compared
       to the prior up-move. This shows selling pressure is weak.

    4. RELATIVE STRENGTH: The stock should be outperforming the S&P 500 over
       the past 20 days. Use a relative strength line or ratio chart.

    5. TRIGGER CANDLE: Enter when a daily candle closes above the high of the
       prior pullback candle with expanding volume. This confirms buyers are
       stepping back in.

ENTRY EXECUTION:
    - Place a buy stop order 0.05 above the trigger candle high
    - If the order is not triggered within 2 days, cancel and re-evaluate
    - Enter at market open only if the gap is less than 2%

THE BREAKDOWN SHORT SETUP (Short Trades):
    - Mirror image of the pullback buy
    - Stock below declining 20-day and 50-day EMAs
    - Bounce into the 10-day or 20-day EMA with decreasing volume
    - Enter on a breakdown candle below the bounce low

================================================================================
    SECTION 3: EXIT RULES AND POSITION MANAGEMENT
================================================================================

STOP LOSS PLACEMENT:
    - Initial stop: Below the pullback low minus 0.10 (for longs)
    - Maximum risk per trade: 1% of total account equity
    - If the stop distance exceeds 7-8% from entry, skip the trade

PROFIT TARGET FRAMEWORK:
    Target 1 (T1): Previous swing high (typically 1:1 risk-to-reward)
    Target 2 (T2): 1.618 Fibonacci extension of the prior swing
    Target 3 (T3): Trail with 10-day EMA for extended moves

POSITION MANAGEMENT RULES:
    - At T1: Sell 1/3 of position, move stop to breakeven
    - At T2: Sell another 1/3, trail stop below 10-day EMA
    - Final 1/3: Hold until 10-day EMA is broken on a closing basis

TIME-BASED EXIT: If the trade has not reached T1 within 10 trading days, close
the position regardless of profit or loss. Dead money has an opportunity cost.

STOP ADJUSTMENT SCHEDULE:
    Day 1-3: Keep initial stop, no adjustments
    Day 4-5: If in profit, tighten stop to below the most recent daily low
    Day 6+: Trail stop below the 5-day EMA on a closing basis

MAXIMUM CONCURRENT POSITIONS: Hold no more than 5-8 swing trades at any time.
Each position should represent 10-15% of your portfolio, with no more than 25%
exposure to any single sector.

================================================================================
    SECTION 4: WEEKLY ROUTINE AND WATCHLIST BUILDING
================================================================================

WEEKEND PREPARATION (Saturday/Sunday - 2 hours):
    1. Review the weekly charts of SPY, QQQ, IWM, and sector ETFs
    2. Determine the market regime (bullish, bearish, choppy)
    3. Scan for pullback setups using a stock screener with these filters:
       - Price above $10, average volume above 500K shares
       - Above 50-day and 200-day moving averages
       - RSI between 40-60 (pulled back but not oversold)
       - Within 5% of the 20-day EMA
    4. Narrow to 10-15 candidates and set alert levels

DAILY ROUTINE (15-30 minutes):
    Morning (before market open):
        - Review pre-market gaps on watchlist stocks
        - Check for overnight news or earnings on holdings
        - Verify all orders are correctly placed

    After market close:
        - Review triggered entries and adjust stops
        - Log all trades in your journal with entry reason
        - Remove stocks from watchlist that have moved past entry zones

MONTHLY PERFORMANCE REVIEW:
    Track these metrics each month:
    - Total trades taken vs. plan adherence rate
    - Win rate (target 50-60%)
    - Average risk-to-reward realized (target 1.5:1 or better)
    - Largest winner and largest loser
    - Portfolio heat (total open risk across all positions)

Consistency in routine creates consistency in results. The swing trading edge
comes not from any single trade, but from disciplined repetition of a
high-probability process over hundreds of trades.

